# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
# Bare *.log per docs/05_GIT_SECURITY_CHECKLIST.md — the create-next-app
# defaults below only cover package-manager logs, and any other log (a build
# dump, a piped dev-server log) is exactly the kind of file that quietly
# carries paths, tokens, or env values into a commit.
*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# agent tooling / working state — NOT project source.
# Agents read these off disk; they don't need to be in git history, and a public
# repo shouldn't be mostly planning artifacts. The project's real planning docs
# are the SEVEN files in /docs, which stay tracked on purpose. (It said five
# until 2026-08-22; `06_INTRO_AND_CHROME.md` and `07_SITE_RESTRUCTURE.md` were
# added after this line was written. Count it, don't restate it.)
# This also covers .claude/settings.local.json, which may hold machine-local config.
#
# `.claude/specs/` IS AN EXCEPTION AND IS TRACKED, since 2026-08-25. Governing
# specs handed down for a piece of work are architecture, not working state, and
# CLAUDE.md's "where decisions live" rule already says a decision constraining a
# later ticket belongs in git. It took a real incident to act on it: a
# git-filter-repo run reset every tracked file and destroyed an uncommitted
# week's work, and `.claude/specs/projects-architecture-spec.md` survived ONLY
# because this rule kept it out of git's reach. The one thing that saved the
# record was the same thing that had kept it unprotected. Not twice.
#
# THE PATTERN IS `.claude/*`, NOT `.claude/`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS LOAD-BEARING.
# Git cannot re-include a path whose PARENT DIRECTORY is excluded, so `.claude/`
# followed by `!.claude/specs/` silently does nothing — the negation never gets
# a chance to match. Excluding the CONTENTS instead leaves the directory itself
# eligible, which is what makes the exception work. Verified with
# `git check-ignore -v` rather than assumed.
#
# `handoff/` stays ignored deliberately: it is 63MB of per-ticket working state
# and screenshots against 144K of specs.
.claude/*
!.claude/specs/
HANDOFF_CONTEXT.md

# MCP server config — may carry credentials if one is ever configured.
# Pre-emptive per docs/05_GIT_SECURITY_CHECKLIST.md: nothing here today, and
# nothing should ever be committed.
.mcp.json
**/.mcp.json
